Chapter: RocIterMap
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 the case named "inspect: iterator-like map uses transformed tag payload".
 Roc builds a LAZY stream as a record holding a thunk, maps over it, and
 reads the first two items:

     Iter(item) :: { next : () -> [One({ item : item, rest : Iter(item) }), Done] }.{
         map : Iter(a), (a -> b) -> Iter(b)
         map = |iter, transform|
             { next: || match (iter.next)() {
                 One({ item, rest }) => One({ item: transform(item), rest: Iter.map(rest, transform) })
                 Done => Done } }
     }
     range_to = |start, end|
         { next: || if start == end { Done } else { One({ item: start, rest: range_to(start + 1, end) }) } }
     mapped = Iter.map(range_to(1, 3), |n| n * 2)
     -- reads first and second, answers first * 10 + second

 and Roc expects 24.

 Two environment adaptations, both named so the port is not taken for a
 loose one.

 The THUNK. Roc's `next` is `() -> ...` and Codex has no nullary function
 type -- a nullary definition is a constant, evaluated once, not a closure
 to enter -- so `next` takes an Integer it ignores. Nothing the case asks
 depends on the argument.

 The PAYLOAD. Roc's `One` carries an anonymous record `{ item, rest }`;
 this carries the two as constructor fields. Codex has no anonymous record
 type, and naming one would test the name rather than the stream.

 What survives both is the whole of what makes this case interesting: the
 stream is a record whose field is a CLOSURE, each closure captures the
 iterator and the transform that built it, the closure outlives the call
 that made it, and entering it recurses into a fresh one. That is findings
 38, 39 and 40's neighbourhood reached three different ways in eight lines.

Section: Types

  Iter (a) = record {
   next : Integer -> Step a
  }

  Step (a) = | One (a) (Iter a) | Done

Section: Functions

  iter-map : Iter a, (a -> b) -> Iter b
  iter-map (it) (transform) =
   Iter { next = \ignored -> when ((it.next) 0)
     is One (item) (rest) -> One (transform item) (iter-map rest transform)
     is Done -> Done }

  range-to : Integer, Integer -> Iter Integer
  range-to (start) (stop) =
   Iter { next = \ignored -> if start == stop then Done
     else One start (range-to (start + 1) stop) }

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let mapped = iter-map (range-to 1 3) (\n -> n * 2)
   in when ((mapped.next) 0)
    is One (first) (rest) -> when ((rest.next) 0)
      is One (second) (ignored) -> print-line-uni (show (first * 10 + second))
      is Done -> print-line-uni (show (0 - 2))
    is Done -> print-line-uni (show (0 - 1))
  end
